WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.962
This example puts WHERE GROUP BY HAVING and ORDER BY
together in

00:00:03.988 --> 00:00:09.425
one realistic pattern The goal is to calculate
category-level sales summaries but

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:14.038
only from rows where price is at least twenty-five
That row-level requirement

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:17.688
belongs in WHERE so lower-priced rows are removed
before the groups are

00:00:17.712 --> 00:00:22.388
created Next GROUP BY category defines the grain as
one row per

00:00:22.412 --> 00:00:27.900
category SUM quantity calculates total units sold
and SUM of quantity times

00:00:27.912 --> 00:00:34.462
price calculates category revenue ROUND makes the
revenue easier to display HAVING

00:00:34.488 --> 00:00:37.988
SUM quantity greater than ten then removes category
groups that do not

00:00:38.012 --> 00:00:42.700
have enough total quantity Notice that this
condition is about a calculated

00:00:42.712 --> 00:00:48.662
group value so HAVING is the correct place Finally
ORDER BY category_revenue

00:00:48.688 --> 00:00:53.538
descending puts the highest qualifying revenue first
Run the query and read

00:00:53.562 --> 00:00:58.188
it in stages Ask which rows enter the calculation
what one output

00:00:58.212 --> 00:01:03.588
row represents what measures are calculated and
which completed groups remain This

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.500
pattern appears constantly in reporting If you
understand this slide well you

00:01:08.525 --> 00:01:12.012
have a strong foundation for many real analytical
SQL tasks
